{"title":"2006 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 24","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_1_(Similar_Triangles)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1 (Similar Triangles)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_2_(Perpendiculars)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2 (Perpendiculars)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Circle\" title=\"Circle\">Circles</a> with centers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/d/51da37d984564162c87710ca27bea422f657fb73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> have radii <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, respectively, and are externally tangent. Points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> on the circle with center <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/d/51da37d984564162c87710ca27bea422f657fb73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> and points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> on the circle with center <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> are such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/5/bc56862b1fdb71204894da278e42ef75de73a40b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> are common external tangents to the circles. What is the area of the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Concave\" title=\"Concave\">concave</a> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Hexagon\" title=\"Hexagon\">hexagon</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/1/6/616e20a79cdf94c3a000d13cf84d5f4191b0603f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AOBCPD$\" width=\"86\" height=\"13\" />?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/4/b/f4b71bfd971e24c4e591a7913e1d83e1fa52420b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy]size(200);defaultpen(linewidth(0.8)); pair X=(-6,0), O=origin, P=(6,0), B=tangent(X, O, 2, 1), A=tangent(X, O, 2, 2), C=tangent(X, P, 4, 1), D=tangent(X, P, 4, 2); pair top=X+15*dir(X--A), bottom=X+15*dir(X--B); draw(Circle(O, 2)^^Circle(P, 4)); draw(bottom--X--top); draw(A--O--B^^O--P^^D--P--C); pair point=X; label(&quot;$2$&quot;, midpoint(O--A), dir(point--midpoint(O--A))); label(&quot;$4$&quot;, midpoint(P--D), dir(point--midpoint(P--D))); label(&quot;$O$&quot;, O, SE); label(&quot;$P$&quot;, P, dir(point--P)); pair point=O; label(&quot;$A$&quot;, A, dir(point--A)); label(&quot;$B$&quot;, B, dir(point--B)); pair point=P; label(&quot;$C$&quot;, C, dir(point--C)); label(&quot;$D$&quot;, D, dir(point--D)); fill((-3,7)--(-3,-7)--(-7,-7)--(-7,7)--cycle, white);[/asy]\" width=\"335\" height=\"275\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/2/212485729f7239cad35f5078b7983b5801d72115.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathrm{(A) \\ } 18\\sqrt{3}\\qquad \\mathrm{(B) \\ } 24\\sqrt{2}\\qquad \\mathrm{(C) \\ } 36\\qquad \\mathrm{(D) \\ } 24\\sqrt{3}\\qquad \\mathrm{(E) \\ } 32\\sqrt{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"485\" height=\"21\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_1\">Video Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/cdjZ9Xd3Yt8\">https://youtu.be/cdjZ9Xd3Yt8</a>\n[Class:Geometry]\n~ Education, the Study of Everything\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_1_.28Similar_Triangles.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1_(Similar_Triangles)\">Solution 1 (Similar Triangles)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1 (Similar Triangles)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>When we see this problem, it practically screams similar triangles at us. Extend <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/d/25dcc9ecb8e7c6fb80766c186721353e6c3dc398.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$OP$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> to the left until it intersects lines <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/5/bc56862b1fdb71204894da278e42ef75de73a40b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> at point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/2/fa2fa899f0afb05d6837885523503a2d4df434f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />. Triangles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/6/1/f615fbbc178c1150def431902f9f0762e2f13537.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EBO$\" width=\"42\" height=\"12\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/d/0cd4f7d5c8220eff8617e3b44e351ff8cf8ea978.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ECP$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"45\" height=\"14\" /> are similar, and by symmetry, so are triangles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/9/4e9f3e586e62d71b9a6a94fc6221544e3240882f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EAO$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/3/3c34c5d2a90d678ab91fc6658e490535235dbd33.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EDP$\" width=\"44\" height=\"12\" />. Then, the area of kite <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/9/2c9b8b6b76ee14a6755df1ae0ae1fb71a4884673.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EAOB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"59\" height=\"14\" /> is just <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/2/3/b2311fdcbcb92371989a727ec839f9b7217dbd78.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4 \\sqrt{2} \\times 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"66\" height=\"21\" /> and the area of kite <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/a/d/cada23044f2a725a5b4620022539ce9a44b35918.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EDPC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/f/76f6c82214de63753f655e8832d72efff68a40e7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8 \\sqrt{2} \\times 4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"66\" height=\"21\" /> (using our similarity ratios). The difference of these yields the area of hexagon <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/1/6/616e20a79cdf94c3a000d13cf84d5f4191b0603f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AOBCPD$\" width=\"86\" height=\"13\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/a/79a59dbcda1154591a7d3878f518cdc17cf85fa4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$24\\sqrt{2} \\Longrightarrow \\boxed{\\mathrm{(B)}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"122\" height=\"32\" />.\n</p><p>~icecreamrolls8\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_2_.28Perpendiculars.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2_(Perpendiculars)\">Solution 2 (Perpendiculars)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2 (Perpendiculars)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>File is too big, so go to <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.imgur.com/a/7aphGaa\">https://www.imgur.com/a/7aphGaa</a>\n</p><p>Sorry for the wrong point names, I didn't know how to change them.\n</p><p>Since a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Tangent_line\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Tangent line\">tangent line</a> is <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Perpendicular\" title=\"Perpendicular\">perpendicular</a> to the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Radius\" title=\"Radius\">radius</a> containing the point of tangency, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/c/63cf6044014daf7595eaba979b816545e71402e0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle OAD = \\angle PDA = 90^\\circ$\" width=\"185\" height=\"13\" />.\n</p><p>Construct a perpendicular to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/3/783d500281f8f46167dff3b071c543d42150e67f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$DP$\" width=\"29\" height=\"12\" /> that goes through point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/d/51da37d984564162c87710ca27bea422f657fb73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />. Label the point of <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Intersection\" title=\"Intersection\">intersection</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>Clearly <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/7/897002d1df1387bf08a161f8e764015fed04ad01.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$OADX$\" width=\"59\" height=\"13\" /> is a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Rectangle\" title=\"Rectangle\">rectangle</a>, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/0/0607a5262ee48d265ee4034142fa1abf2ce6ccc4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$DX=2$\" width=\"65\" height=\"12\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/1/3/f1365d76f054950658af9897482a59d06a295e8f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PX=2$\" width=\"63\" height=\"12\" />. By the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Pythagorean_Theorem\" title=\"Pythagorean Theorem\">Pythagorean Theorem</a>, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/3/1/2314d76c46b68521c3f9680d16bcd647498bcba4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$OX = \\sqrt{6^2 - 2^2} = 4\\sqrt{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"183\" height=\"19\" />.\n</p><p>The area of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/7/897002d1df1387bf08a161f8e764015fed04ad01.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$OADX$\" width=\"59\" height=\"13\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/c/a6cb58e6357eea4fa72631d15cfa0cf7567f7dad.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2\\cdot4\\sqrt{2}=8\\sqrt{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"115\" height=\"21\" />. The area of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/7/a/d7a100b88e2645498347bc5c54b5283adac52636.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$OXP$\" width=\"45\" height=\"12\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/0/210df524797baf8e1cd46c76b89cec99bd9e44e0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot2\\cdot4\\sqrt{2}=4\\sqrt{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"139\" height=\"37\" />, so the area of quadrilateral <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/5/7754aafadd7894f4d62ff63e9490ddda7a30f8a2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$OADP$\" width=\"57\" height=\"13\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/d/d/4dd469531d07eeac2c9e3466ad2e961db169210b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8\\sqrt{2}+4\\sqrt{2}=12\\sqrt{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"156\" height=\"18\" />. Using similar steps, the area of quadrilateral <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/b/78b6738f765ab3ecf47499d080ef7bc63ea4e9f4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$OBCP$\" width=\"57\" height=\"12\" /> is also <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/f/34f7a9875557179ece650016df1cc310df671c71.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$12\\sqrt{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"43\" height=\"18\" />. Therefore, the area of hexagon <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/1/6/616e20a79cdf94c3a000d13cf84d5f4191b0603f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AOBCPD$\" width=\"86\" height=\"13\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/d/9/2d97747641089dd0c4ac4364e38cc2beb909d16c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2\\cdot12\\sqrt{2}= 24\\sqrt{2} \\Longrightarrow \\boxed{\\mathrm{(B)}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"209\" height=\"29\" />.\n</p><p><br />\nNote: Quadrilaterals <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/5/7754aafadd7894f4d62ff63e9490ddda7a30f8a2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$OADP$\" width=\"57\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/b/78b6738f765ab3ecf47499d080ef7bc63ea4e9f4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$OBCP$\" width=\"57\" height=\"12\" /> are congruent, so they have equal areas.\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/8/6/18663816654e026de44a2e4f26ae5cd5f17a5ff2.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] size(250); import geometry; // Required for markrightangle  // Centers and Radii pair O = (0,0); pair P = (6,0); real r1 = 2; real r2 = 4;  // The external intersection point X of the common tangents // Found by: X = O - (P-O) * r1/(r2-r1) = (0,0) - (6,0) * 2/2 = (-6,0) pair X = (-6,0);  // Tangent points using the point X pair A = tangent(X, O, r1, 2); pair B = tangent(X, O, r1, 1); pair D = tangent(X, P, r2, 2); pair C = tangent(X, P, r2, 1);  // Point E is on PD such that OE is perpendicular to PD (parallel to AD) // This creates segment DE = 2, so EP = 4. pair E = interp(D, P, 0.5);   // Drawing draw(circle(O, r1)); draw(circle(P, r2));  // Extend the tangent lines from X draw(interp(X, A, 2.5) -- X -- interp(X, B, 2.5));  // Radii and center line draw(A--O--B); draw(D--P--C); draw(O--P);  // The requested segment OE draw(O--E, grey+0.8pt); markrightangle(O, E, P, size=4);  // Labels for points label(&quot;$O$&quot;, O, SW); label(&quot;$P$&quot;, P, SE); label(&quot;$A$&quot;, A, NW); label(&quot;$B$&quot;, B, SW); label(&quot;$C$&quot;, C, S); label(&quot;$D$&quot;, D, N); label(&quot;$E$&quot;, E, NE);  // Length labels as requested label(&quot;$2$&quot;, midpoint(O--A), W); label(&quot;$4$&quot;, midpoint(E--P), E); label(&quot;$6$&quot;, midpoint(O--P), S); label(&quot;$4\\sqrt{2}$&quot;, midpoint(O--E), N); label(&quot;$4\\sqrt{2}$&quot;, midpoint(B--C), S);[/asy]\" width=\"418\" height=\"248\" />\n</p><p>Draw a line through point O parallel to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/5/bc56862b1fdb71204894da278e42ef75de73a40b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> and call the point where this line meets <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/3/783d500281f8f46167dff3b071c543d42150e67f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$DP$\" width=\"29\" height=\"12\" /> E. Note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/9/2/9920fa380c78e3d420e6cb227a67abfc2a8301dc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$OP=6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"64\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/0/c/80c5b343e3586bf863fdd6d5b4d2da1afb4e8b16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EP=2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"64\" height=\"14\" />. By the Pythagorean Theorem, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/f/79f7655974a489002811a73d76edf5a12168f96f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$OE=4\\sqrt{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"88\" height=\"21\" />. We created rectangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/a/9/5a95f497ae6fd05ff714d41560dad5914de5a6e2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$DAOE$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\" /> and thus <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/7/6/d76055038819b07eaa8f42e1140914ea3c809c38.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AD=OE=4\\sqrt{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"142\" height=\"21\" />. Now we know height and both top and bottom heights. The area of a trapezoid is given by: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/c/2ec1d2657a82e8d6a43655122755385087f4d55e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$K=\\frac{b_1+b_2}{2}\\cdot h$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"121\" height=\"37\" />, where the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8136a7ef6a03334a7246df9097e5bcc31ba33fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"14\" />s are the parallel sides and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/8/8189a5b5a0917b8c93350827be4038af1839139d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$h$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> is the distance between those bases. Plug stuff in to get that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/e/a/feaa203f2b3dbd03f76f0fc1fd56001c4a6dc198.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[ADPO]=12\\sqrt{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"136\" height=\"24\" />. Remember to multiply by two because there are two of these equal trapezoids, and you get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/d/9/2d97747641089dd0c4ac4364e38cc2beb909d16c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2\\cdot12\\sqrt{2}= 24\\sqrt{2} \\Longrightarrow \\boxed{\\mathrm{(B)}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"209\" height=\"29\" />.\n</p><p>my asymptote thingy makes the two lines meet can somebody fix that im too lazy to\n</p><p>~dw (fp77)\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution\">Video Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EmUfNAxLZ7A\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EmUfNAxLZ7A</a>    ~David\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_AMC_10B\" title=\"2006 AMC 10B\">2006 AMC 10B</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_AMC_10B_Problems\" title=\"2006 AMC 10B Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_AMC_10B_Answer_Key\" title=\"2006 AMC 10B Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c13\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_23\" title=\"2006 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 23\">Problem 23</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" ali